  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA stationary front remains draped across the area this \nafternoon with an upper-low anchoring it down and producing \nshowers and thunderstorms through the afternoon today. These \nshowers and storms will be relatively slow in speed, moving from\nsouth to north. Severe weather is not anticipated, but isolated\ninstances of damaging wind gusts could be possible in more\norganized cells, mostly a concern for today and Thursday. The\nlow chance for severe weather looks promising into next week,\nthough would still be mindful of isolated damaging wind gusts\nmostly across the mountains with the upslope potential. Because\nof this, would not be surprised to see a marginal risk\n(level 1/5) from SPC being introduced tomorrow with soundings\nsuggesting possible localized wet microburst potential,\nespecially across the mountains and Ohio River Valley.\n\nThe main concern today, and really the next few days, will be\nlocalized to isolated flash flooding from heavy downpours and\nslow/training storms. A moisture gradient moves through today\ninto tomorrow with PWATs increasing to between 1.75\" and 2.25\".\nThere is a marginal risk (level 1/4) for excessive rainfall\nacross our area virtually everyday through the weekend. Current\nWPC guidance shows 0.50\" and 1.50\" of rainfall being picked up\nacross the area through Friday night. Locally higher amounts\nclose to 2 inches is possible across the mountains and Ohio\nRiver Valley. Higher amounts are likely to occur with training\nthunderstorms and showers, however confidence is lower as to\nwhere these will occur. That said, a flood watch is not\nanticipated at this time due to the more localized threat\nexpected.\n\nThis trend of daily rounds of showers and thunderstorms will \nhold through the weekend into next week, until a cold front\nswings through mid week.",
